  "prediction": "**Seven Men Charged in 2014 Hazing Death, Fraternity Sues for Unauthorized Use of Name**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, seven men have been charged in connection with the 2014 hazing death of University at Albany student Trevor Duffy. The men, accused of forcing Duffy to drink vodka leading to his death, were allegedly part of a group that used the name of the Zeta Beta Tau (ZBT) fraternity without authorization.\n\nThe ZBT fraternity has released a statement denouncing the group and announcing plans to sue the individuals involved. According to the statement, the group was not recognized by the university or the national fraternity and falsely represented themselves as part of the Epsilon Gamma chapter of ZBT.\n\nThe national fraternity has retained counsel to bring a lawsuit against the individuals, claiming they falsely represented themselves, deceived the public, and damaged the fraternity's reputation. ZBT officials stated that the unauthorized use of their name and symbols is protected by copyright and leaves the rogue group subject to legal action.\n\nThe lawsuit comes as a surprise to the defense team of Joseph Angilletta, one of the students accused in connection with Duffy's death. Angilletta's attorney, Paul DerOhannesian, expressed skepticism about the motives behind the lawsuit, stating that it is \"novel\" and \"different\" from previous actions taken by the fraternity.\n\nThe ZBT fraternity, founded in 1898 in New York City, has over 130,000 members worldwide and chapters on more than 90 campuses. The fraternity has a long history of promoting Jewish values and community service.\n\nThe charges against the seven men are a significant development in the case, and the lawsuit filed by the ZBT fraternity adds another layer of complexity to the situation. As the case unfolds, it remains to be seen how the fraternity's actions will impact the investigation and the families of those involved.",
